export default {"id":"sounddesign-level-7","number":7,"title":"Subtractive Mastery","skillBand":"Textbook","duration":"From the course textbook","summary":"- Master the architectural layout of Ableton's native subtractive synthesizer: Drift. - Control the integrated Drift (Pitch Instability) circuit to simulate vintage analog oscillator drift.","modules":[{"id":"sounddesign-m-7-1-1","code":"7.1","title":"Drift as Fast Lab","blurb":"- Master the architectural layout of Ableton's native subtractive synthesizer: Drift. - Control the integrated Drift (Pitch Instability) circuit to simulate vintage analog oscillator drift.","outcomes":[],"sources":[],"practiceFocus":[],"checkpoints":[],"lessons":[{"id":"sounddesign-l-o-19","title":"Section overview","blocks":[{"type":"step","title":"Learning Objectives","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["Master the architectural layout of Ableton's native subtractive synthesizer: <strong>Drift</strong>.","Control the integrated <strong>Drift (Pitch Instability)</strong> circuit to simulate vintage analog oscillator drift.","Construct five production-grade patches from an initialized (<code>init</code>) state:","<code>SD_Drift_PunchySubBass</code>","<code>SD_Drift_ClassicSawLead</code>","<code>SD_Drift_WarmPWMPad</code>","<code>SD_Drift_PercussivePluck</code>","<code>SD_Drift_LaserZapFX</code>","Save and categorize custom user patches into the Ableton User Library."],"ordered":false}]}],"minutes":8},{"id":"sounddesign-l-7-1-1","title":"7.1.1 — Drift Panel Anatomy","blocks":[{"type":"step","title":"7.1.1 — Drift Panel Anatomy","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"<code>Drift</code> is designed for rapid subtractive workflow with zero nested menus:"},{"type":"prose","html":"<pre style=\"white-space:pre-wrap;font-family:ui-monospace,monospace\">+---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------+\n|                                      ABLETON DRIFT ARCHITECTURE                                   |\n+---------------------+---------------------+---------------------+---------------------------------+\n| OSCILLATOR SECTION  | FILTER SECTION      | ENVELOPE SECTION    | LFO &amp; MODULATION                |\n+---------------------+---------------------+---------------------+---------------------------------+\n| - Osc 1: Shape, Oct | - Mode: LP12, LP24, | - Env 1 (Amp ADSR)  | - LFO: Wave, Rate (Hz/Sync)     |\n| - Osc 2: Shape, Semi|   HP12, BP12, Notch | - Env 2 (Mod ADSR)  | - Mod Matrix: Pitch, Cutoff,    |\n|   Detune, Gain      | - Cutoff (fc) &amp; Res | - Velocity Routing  |   Pulse Width, Volume           |\n| - Noise Generator   | - Morph / HP Filter | - Cyl / Mod Toggles | - Voice Mode: Poly/Mono/Unison  |\n+---------------------+---------------------+---------------------+---------------------------------+</pre>"},{"type":"prose","html":"<strong>Sources:</strong>"},{"type":"list","items":["<em>Ableton Live 12 Reference Manual</em>, Chapter 23 (Drift Reference)","<em>Ableton Blog: Drift — Exploring the New Synth</em>"],"ordered":false}]}],"minutes":8},{"id":"sounddesign-l-7-1-2","title":"7.1.2 — The Drift Control: Analog Pitch Instability","blocks":[{"type":"step","title":"7.1.2 — The Drift Control: Analog Pitch Instability","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"Real analog synthesizer circuits exhibit subtle microscopic pitch fluctuations due to temperature changes in hardware transistors and capacitors."},{"type":"prose","html":"The <strong>Drift Control</strong> in <code>Drift</code> injects mathematically modeled, independent analog pitch and filter drift across every voice:"},{"type":"list","items":["<strong>Drift = 0.0:</strong> Perfect digital tuning stability.","<strong>Drift = 2.0 - 5.0:</strong> Subtle vintage acoustic warmth; softens digital harshness and creates natural movement between polyphonic voices.","<strong>Drift &gt; 8.0:</strong> Severe out-of-tune warble (vintage tape / broken synth aesthetic)."],"ordered":false},{"type":"prose","html":"<strong>Sources:</strong>"},{"type":"list","items":["<em>Ableton Live 12 Reference Manual</em>, Chapter 23 (Drift Parameter Reference)"],"ordered":false}]}],"minutes":8},{"id":"sounddesign-l-7-1-3","title":"7.1.3 — Patch 1 from Init: Punchy Sub-Bass","blocks":[{"type":"step","title":"7.1.3 — Patch 1 from Init: Punchy Sub-Bass","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"<pre style=\"white-space:pre-wrap;font-family:ui-monospace,monospace\">+-------------------------------------------------------------------------------+\n| PATCH SPECIFICATION: PUNCHY SUB-BASS                                          |\n+------------------+------------------------------------------------------------+\n| **Osc 1**        | Shape: **Sine**, Octave: **-1**                            |\n| **Osc 2**        | Off                                                        |\n| **Filter**       | Low-Pass **LP24**, Cutoff: **180 Hz**, Resonance: **0.0**  |\n| **Env 1 (Amp)**  | Attack: **0 ms**, Decay: **400 ms**, Sustain: **0.75**,    |\n|                  | Release: **30 ms**                                         |\n| **Env 2 (Mod)**  | Attack: **0 ms**, Decay: **20 ms**, Sustain: **0.0**,       |\n|                  | Destination: **Pitch**, Amount: **+18.00 st**              |\n| **Voice Mode**   | **Mono**, Glide: **Off**                                   |\n+------------------+------------------------------------------------------------+</pre>"},{"type":"prose","html":"<em>Result:</em> A rock-solid mono sub-bass with an explosive 20 ms transient pitch click on note onset."},{"type":"prose","html":"<strong>Sources:</strong>"},{"type":"list","items":["<em>Sound-Design-Beginner-Knowledgebase.md</em>, §12 (Recipe 1)"],"ordered":false}]}],"minutes":8},{"id":"sounddesign-l-7-1-4","title":"7.1.4 — Patch 2 from Init: Classic Saw Lead","blocks":[{"type":"step","title":"7.1.4 — Patch 2 from Init: Classic Saw Lead","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"<pre style=\"white-space:pre-wrap;font-family:ui-monospace,monospace\">+-------------------------------------------------------------------------------+\n| PATCH SPECIFICATION: CLASSIC SAW GLIDE LEAD                                  |\n+------------------+------------------------------------------------------------+\n| **Osc 1**        | Shape: **Sawtooth**, Octave: **0**                         |\n| **Osc 2**        | Shape: **Sawtooth**, Octave: **0**, Detune: **+8 cents**   |\n| **Filter**       | Low-Pass **LP12**, Cutoff: **2.5 kHz**, Resonance: **0.30**,Keytrack: **100%**|\n| **Env 1 (Amp)**  | Attack: **2 ms**, Decay: **800 ms**, Sustain: **0.80**,    |\n|                  | Release: **120 ms**                                        |\n| **LFO**          | Shape: **Sine**, Rate: **5.5 Hz** (Hz Mode), Depth: **0.0**|\n|                  | Route **Mod Wheel** to LFO Depth (+30%)                    |\n| **Voice Mode**   | **Mono**, Mode: **Legato**, Glide Time: **65 ms**          |\n+------------------+------------------------------------------------------------+</pre>"},{"type":"prose","html":"<em>Result:</em> A soaring, cutting monophonic lead that glides smoothly across overlapping notes with mod-wheel vibrato control."},{"type":"prose","html":"<strong>Sources:</strong>"},{"type":"list","items":["<em>Sound-Design-Beginner-Knowledgebase.md</em>, §12 (Recipe D)"],"ordered":false}]}],"minutes":8},{"id":"sounddesign-l-7-1-5","title":"7.1.5 — Patch 3 from Init: Warm PWM Pad","blocks":[{"type":"step","title":"7.1.5 — Patch 3 from Init: Warm PWM Pad","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"<pre style=\"white-space:pre-wrap;font-family:ui-monospace,monospace\">+-------------------------------------------------------------------------------+\n| PATCH SPECIFICATION: WARM PWM STEREO PAD                                      |\n+------------------+------------------------------------------------------------+\n| **Osc 1**        | Shape: **Pulse**, Octave: **0**                            |\n| **Osc 2**        | Shape: **Triangle**, Octave: **-1**, Level: **-4 dB**      |\n| **Noise**        | Level: **-22 dB** (Subtle breath)                          |\n| **Filter**       | Low-Pass **LP12**, Cutoff: **650 Hz**, Resonance: **0.15** |\n| **Env 1 (Amp)**  | Attack: **800 ms**, Decay: **1.5 s**, Sustain: **0.90**,   |\n|                  | Release: **2.0 s**                                         |\n| **Env 2 (Mod)**  | Attack: **1.2 s**, Decay: **2.0 s**, Sustain: **0.60**,    |\n|                  | Destination: **Filter Cutoff**, Amount: **+3.50**          |\n| **LFO**          | Shape: **Triangle**, Rate: **0.35 Hz**, Destination: **Osc 1 PW**, Depth: **0.70**|\n| **Voice Mode**   | **Stereo (Poly)**, Voices: **6**, Drift: **4.5**           |\n+------------------+------------------------------------------------------------+</pre>"},{"type":"prose","html":"<em>Result:</em> A lush, evolving ambient pad with rich internal PWM phasing and slow filter blooming."},{"type":"prose","html":"<strong>Sources:</strong>"},{"type":"list","items":["<em>Sound-Design-Beginner-Knowledgebase.md</em>, §12 (Recipe A)"],"ordered":false}]}],"minutes":8},{"id":"sounddesign-l-7-1-6","title":"7.1.6 — Patch 4 from Init: Percussive Pluck","blocks":[{"type":"step","title":"7.1.6 — Patch 4 from Init: Percussive Pluck","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"<pre style=\"white-space:pre-wrap;font-family:ui-monospace,monospace\">+-------------------------------------------------------------------------------+\n| PATCH SPECIFICATION: PERCUSSIVE PLUCK KEY                                     |\n+------------------+------------------------------------------------------------+\n| **Osc 1**        | Shape: **Square**, Octave: **0**                           |\n| **Osc 2**        | Shape: **Sawtooth**, Octave: **+1**, Level: **-6 dB**      |\n| **Filter**       | Low-Pass **LP24**, Cutoff: **280 Hz**, Resonance: **0.40** |\n| **Env 1 (Amp)**  | Attack: **0 ms**, Decay: **280 ms**, Sustain: **0.0**,     |\n|                  | Release: **80 ms**                                         |\n| **Env 2 (Mod)**  | Attack: **0 ms**, Decay: **180 ms**, Sustain: **0.0**,     |\n|                  | Destination: **Filter Cutoff**, Amount: **+7.50**          |\n| **Voice Mode**   | **Poly**, Voices: **8**                                    |\n+------------------+------------------------------------------------------------+</pre>"},{"type":"prose","html":"<em>Result:</em> A tight, punchy, hollow pluck suitable for fast melodic arpeggiator patterns."},{"type":"prose","html":"<strong>Sources:</strong>"},{"type":"list","items":["<em>Sound-Design-Beginner-Knowledgebase.md</em>, §12 (Recipe B)"],"ordered":false}]}],"minutes":8},{"id":"sounddesign-l-7-1-7","title":"7.1.7 — Patch 5 from Init: Laser / Zap FX","blocks":[{"type":"step","title":"7.1.7 — Patch 5 from Init: Laser / Zap FX","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"<pre style=\"white-space:pre-wrap;font-family:ui-monospace,monospace\">+-------------------------------------------------------------------------------+\n| PATCH SPECIFICATION: SYNTHETIC LASER / ZAP FX                                 |\n+------------------+------------------------------------------------------------+\n| **Osc 1**        | Shape: **Sawtooth**, Octave: **+1**                        |\n| **Osc 2**        | Off                                                        |\n| **Filter**       | Band-Pass **BP12**, Cutoff: **1.8 kHz**, Resonance: **0.75**|\n| **Env 1 (Amp)**  | Attack: **0 ms**, Decay: **140 ms**, Sustain: **0.0**,     |\n|                  | Release: **20 ms**                                         |\n| **Env 2 (Mod)**  | Attack: **0 ms**, Decay: **45 ms**, Sustain: **0.0**,      |\n|                  | Destination: **Pitch**, Amount: **+48.00 st**              |\n| **Voice Mode**   | **Mono**                                                   |\n+------------------+------------------------------------------------------------+</pre>"},{"type":"prose","html":"<em>Result:</em> A classic arcade laser zap driven by an extreme 4-octave (+48 semitone) pitch envelope drop in 45 ms."},{"type":"prose","html":"<strong>Sources:</strong>"},{"type":"list","items":["<em>Ultimate Sound Design: Mastering Sound Design</em>, Section 6.11 (Synthesizing Sound Effects)"],"ordered":false}]}],"minutes":8},{"id":"sounddesign-l-7-1-8","title":"7.1.8 — Saving All 5 to User Library","blocks":[{"type":"step","title":"7.1.8 — Saving All 5 to User Library","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"Save each created patch to your User Library under <code>Presets/Instruments/Drift/</code> using standard naming:"},{"type":"list","items":["<code>SD_Drift_PunchySubBass.adv</code>","<code>SD_Drift_ClassicSawLead.adv</code>","<code>SD_Drift_WarmPWMPad.adv</code>","<code>SD_Drift_PercussivePluck.adv</code>","<code>SD_Drift_LaserZapFX.adv</code>"],"ordered":true},{"type":"prose","html":"You have now established an initialized subtractive reference bank."},{"type":"prose","html":"<strong>Sources:</strong>"},{"type":"list","items":["<em>Sound-Design-Beginner-Knowledgebase.md</em>, §11 &amp; §14"],"ordered":false}]}],"minutes":8}],"exercises":[]},{"id":"sounddesign-m-7-2-2","code":"7.2","title":"Analog Deep Practice","blurb":"- Master the dual-signal-path architecture of Ableton's virtual analog instrument: Analog. - Implement Serial and Parallel Filter Routing across two independent oscillators, filters, and amplifiers.","outcomes":[],"sources":[],"practiceFocus":[],"checkpoints":[],"lessons":[{"id":"sounddesign-l-o-20","title":"Section overview","blocks":[{"type":"step","title":"Learning Objectives","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["Master the dual-signal-path architecture of Ableton's virtual analog instrument: <strong>Analog</strong>.","Implement <strong>Serial</strong> and <strong>Parallel Filter Routing</strong> across two independent oscillators, filters, and amplifiers.","Integrate the dedicated hardware-modeled <strong>Sub-Oscillator</strong> and <strong>Noise Generator</strong>.","Construct four production-standard vintage analog patches from an initialized (<code>init</code>) state:","<code>SD_Analog_DetroitTechnoStab</code>","<code>SD_Analog_VintageReeseBass</code>","<code>SD_Analog_80sPolyBrass</code>","<code>SD_Analog_SH_ComputerChatterFX</code>","Save and categorize custom user patches into the Ableton User Library."],"ordered":false}]}],"minutes":8},{"id":"sounddesign-l-7-2-1","title":"7.2.1 — Analog Panel Anatomy","blocks":[{"type":"step","title":"7.2.1 — Analog Panel Anatomy","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"<code>Analog</code> is a dual-oscillator, dual-filter virtual analog synthesizer modeled after vintage discrete electronic hardware:"},{"type":"prose","html":"<pre style=\"white-space:pre-wrap;font-family:ui-monospace,monospace\">+---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------+\n|                                      ABLETON ANALOG ARCHITECTURE                                  |\n+---------------------+---------------------+---------------------+---------------------------------+\n| OSCILLATOR 1 &amp; 2    | FILTER 1 &amp; 2        | AMPLIFIER 1 &amp; 2     | GLOBAL / LFO SECTION            |\n+---------------------+---------------------+---------------------+---------------------------------+\n| - Shape: Saw, Square| - Mode: LP24, LP12, | - Amp ADSR Envelopes| - LFO 1 &amp; LFO 2                 |\n|   Sine, White Noise |   HP24, HP12, BP, N | - Pan &amp; Level       | - Global Pitch / Vibrato        |\n| - Octave, Semi, Fine| - Cutoff &amp; Res      | - Drive Curves:     | - Unison &amp; Detune               |\n| - Sub-Oscillator    | - F1/F2 Balance     |   Sym1, Sym2,       | - Glide &amp; Voice Priority        |\n| - Sync Mode         | - Filter Envelopes  |   Asym1, Pos1       | - Polyphony ($1 - 32\\text{ v}$) |\n+---------------------+---------------------+---------------------+---------------------------------+</pre>"},{"type":"prose","html":"Clicking any section header (e.g., <code>Osc 1</code>, <code>Fil 1</code>, <code>Amp 1</code>) loads that specific module's detailed parameter display directly in the center display screen."},{"type":"prose","html":"<strong>Sources:</strong>"},{"type":"list","items":["<em>Ableton Live 12 Reference Manual</em>, Chapter 23 (Analog Reference)","<em>Sound-Design-Beginner-Knowledgebase.md</em>, §6.2"],"ordered":false}]}],"minutes":8},{"id":"sounddesign-l-7-2-2","title":"7.2.2 — Dual Filter Routing: Serial vs. Parallel Execution","blocks":[{"type":"step","title":"7.2.2 — Dual Filter Routing: Serial vs. Parallel Execution","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"<code>Analog</code> allows complete freedom over how the two oscillators connect to the two filters:"},{"type":"prose","html":"<pre style=\"white-space:pre-wrap;font-family:ui-monospace,monospace\">PARALLEL CONFIGURATION (Two Independent Sounds):\n[ OSC 1 (Sawtooth) ] =======&gt; [ FILTER 1 (Low-Pass 24 dB) ] =======&gt; [ AMP 1 ] ===+\n                                                                                  +===&gt; MASTER OUT\n[ OSC 2 (Square)   ] =======&gt; [ FILTER 2 (Band-Pass 12 dB) ] ======&gt; [ AMP 2 ] ===+\n\nSERIAL CONFIGURATION (Cascading Dual Filters):\n[ OSC 1 &amp; 2 ] ===&gt; [ FILTER 1 (Warm Low-Pass) ] ===(To F2 = 100%)===&gt; [ FILTER 2 (High-Pass Cut) ] ===&gt; AMP 2</pre>"},{"type":"list","items":["<strong>In the central Filter 1 display:</strong> Set <code>To F2</code> to 100% to cascade Filter 1 directly into Filter 2.","<strong>In the Oscillator display:</strong> Use the <code>F1/F2</code> slider to balance how much signal is routed to each filter."],"ordered":false},{"type":"prose","html":"<strong>Sources:</strong>"},{"type":"list","items":["<em>Ableton Live 12 Reference Manual</em>, Chapter 23 (Analog Routing Section)"],"ordered":false}]}],"minutes":8},{"id":"sounddesign-l-7-2-3","title":"7.2.3 — Sub-Oscillator Integration","blocks":[{"type":"step","title":"7.2.3 — Sub-Oscillator Integration","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"Each oscillator in <code>Analog</code> features an integrated, pitch-locked <strong>Sub-Oscillator</strong>:"},{"type":"prose","html":"<pre style=\"white-space:pre-wrap;font-family:ui-monospace,monospace\">[ MAIN OSC 1 (Sawtooth @ 8') ] ===(Mix Level: 100%)===&gt; [ FILTER 1 ]\n               +\n[ SUB-OSC 1  (Square @ 16')  ] ===(Sub Level: 70%)====&gt; [ FILTER 1 ]</pre>"},{"type":"list","items":["Sub-Oscillator modes include <strong>-1 Octave (16')</strong> or <strong>-2 Octaves (32')</strong>.","Waveforms include <strong>Square</strong> (hollow, cutting weight) or <strong>Linear Triangle</strong> (pure, warm fundamental).","<strong>Application:</strong> Keeps solo leads and acid basses anchored to a heavy low-end fundamental even when the main oscillator is sweeping high in pitch."],"ordered":false},{"type":"prose","html":"<strong>Sources:</strong>"},{"type":"list","items":["<em>Ableton Live 12 Reference Manual</em>, Chapter 23 (Analog Oscillator Parameters)"],"ordered":false}]}],"minutes":8},{"id":"sounddesign-l-7-2-4","title":"7.2.4 — Patch 1 from Init: Detroit Techno Stab","blocks":[{"type":"step","title":"7.2.4 — Patch 1 from Init: Detroit Techno Stab","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"<pre style=\"white-space:pre-wrap;font-family:ui-monospace,monospace\">+-------------------------------------------------------------------------------+\n| PATCH SPECIFICATION: DETROIT TECHNO CHORD STAB                                |\n+------------------+------------------------------------------------------------+\n| **Osc 1**        | Shape: **Sawtooth**, Octave: **0**, Semi: **0 st**         |\n| **Osc 2**        | Shape: **Sawtooth**, Octave: **0**, Semi: **+7 st** (5th), Detune: **+5c**|\n| **Filter 1**     | Low-Pass **LP24**, Cutoff: **380 Hz**, Resonance: **0.45**, Keytrack: **100%**|\n| **Fil 1 Env**    | Attack: **0 ms**, Decay: **220 ms**, Sustain: **0.0**,     |\n|                  | Env Amount: **+6.50**                                      |\n| **Amp 1**        | Attack: **0 ms**, Decay: **320 ms**, Sustain: **0.0**,     |\n|                  | Release: **50 ms**                                         |\n| **Voices**       | **Mono**, Glide: **Off**                                   |\n+------------------+------------------------------------------------------------+</pre>"},{"type":"prose","html":"<em>Result:</em> A dark, aggressive, parallel-5th chord stab that punches through industrial techno arrangements."},{"type":"prose","html":"<strong>Sources:</strong>"},{"type":"list","items":["<em>Ultimate Sound Design: Mastering Sound Design</em>, Section 6.12 (Techno Stabs)","<em>Sound-Design-Beginner-Knowledgebase.md</em>, §12"],"ordered":false}]}],"minutes":8},{"id":"sounddesign-l-7-2-5","title":"7.2.5 — Patch 2 from Init: Vintage Reese Bass","blocks":[{"type":"step","title":"7.2.5 — Patch 2 from Init: Vintage Reese Bass","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"<pre style=\"white-space:pre-wrap;font-family:ui-monospace,monospace\">+-------------------------------------------------------------------------------+\n| PATCH SPECIFICATION: VINTAGE REESE BASS                                       |\n+------------------+------------------------------------------------------------+\n| **Osc 1**        | Shape: **Sawtooth**, Octave: **-1**, Detune: **-8 cents**   |\n| **Osc 2**        | Shape: **Sawtooth**, Octave: **-1**, Detune: **+8 cents**   |\n| **Filter 1**     | Low-Pass **LP24**, Cutoff: **450 Hz**, Resonance: **0.30** |\n| **Fil 1 Env**    | Attack: **0 ms**, Decay: **600 ms**, Sustain: **0.40**,    |\n|                  | Env Amount: **+2.50**                                      |\n| **Amp 1**        | Attack: **2 ms**, Decay: **1.0 s**, Sustain: **0.80**,     |\n|                  | Release: **40 ms**                                         |\n| **Amp 1 Drive**  | Type: **Sym1** (Analog Overdrive Saturation)               |\n| **Voices**       | **Mono**, Mode: **Legato**, Glide Time: **50 ms**          |\n+------------------+------------------------------------------------------------+</pre>"},{"type":"prose","html":"<em>Result:</em> The foundational Drum &amp; Bass / Jungle Reese bassline: two detuned saws creating 16 cent phase beating, filtered and driven through a warm 24 dB low-pass filter."},{"type":"prose","html":"<strong>Sources:</strong>"},{"type":"list","items":["<em>Drum-and-Bass-Production-Knowledgebase.md</em>, §6.2 (Classic Reese in Standard)","<em>Sound-Design-Beginner-Knowledgebase.md</em>, §12 (Recipe 2)"],"ordered":false}]}],"minutes":8},{"id":"sounddesign-l-7-2-6","title":"7.2.6 — Patch 3 from Init: 80s Polyphonic Brass","blocks":[{"type":"step","title":"7.2.6 — Patch 3 from Init: 80s Polyphonic Brass","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"<pre style=\"white-space:pre-wrap;font-family:ui-monospace,monospace\">+-------------------------------------------------------------------------------+\n| PATCH SPECIFICATION: 80s SYNTH POLY BRASS                                     |\n+------------------+------------------------------------------------------------+\n| **Osc 1**        | Shape: **Sawtooth**, Octave: **0**, Semi: **0 st**         |\n| **Osc 2**        | Shape: **Sawtooth**, Octave: **0**, Detune: **+10 cents**  |\n| **Filter 1**     | Low-Pass **LP24**, Cutoff: **500 Hz**, Resonance: **0.20**,Keytrack: **100%**|\n| **Fil 1 Env**    | Attack: **45 ms** (Brass swell), Decay: **800 ms**,        |\n|                  | Sustain: **0.50**, Env Amount: **+5.00**                   |\n| **Amp 1**        | Attack: **15 ms**, Decay: **1.0 s**, Sustain: **0.75**,    |\n|                  | Release: **250 ms**                                        |\n| **Unison**       | Voices: **2**, Detune: **15 cents**                        |\n| **Voices**       | **Poly**, Voices: **8**                                    |\n+------------------+------------------------------------------------------------+</pre>"},{"type":"prose","html":"<em>Result:</em> A classic, swelling 1980s polyphonic synthesizer brass chord sound (modeled after the Prophet-5 and Jupiter-8)."},{"type":"prose","html":"<strong>Sources:</strong>"},{"type":"list","items":["<em>Ultimate Sound Design: Mastering Sound Design</em>, Section 6.13 (Synthesizing Brass)"],"ordered":false}]}],"minutes":8},{"id":"sounddesign-l-7-2-7","title":"7.2.7 — Patch 4 from Init: S&H Computer Chatter FX","blocks":[{"type":"step","title":"7.2.7 — Patch 4 from Init: S&H Computer Chatter FX","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"<pre style=\"white-space:pre-wrap;font-family:ui-monospace,monospace\">+-------------------------------------------------------------------------------+\n| PATCH SPECIFICATION: S&amp;H SCI-FI COMPUTER CHATTER                              |\n+------------------+------------------------------------------------------------+\n| **Osc 1**        | Shape: **White Noise**                                     |\n| **Osc 2**        | Shape: **Sine**, Octave: **+1**                            |\n| **Filter 1**     | Band-Pass **BP12**, Cutoff: **1.5 kHz**, Resonance: **0.85**|\n| **LFO 1**        | Shape: **S&amp;H (Sample &amp; Hold)**, Rate: **7.5 Hz** (Hz Mode) |\n|                  | Route LFO 1 to: **Fil 1 Cutoff (+80%)** and **Osc 2 Pitch (+24 st)**|\n| **Amp 1**        | Attack: **0 ms**, Decay: **10 s**, Sustain: **100%** (Drone)|\n| **Voices**       | **Mono**                                                   |\n+------------------+------------------------------------------------------------+</pre>"},{"type":"prose","html":"<em>Result:</em> Classic retro-futuristic modular computer chatter: a stepped random S&amp;H LFO continuously jumping across resonant bandpass frequencies and pitch steps."},{"type":"prose","html":"<strong>Sources:</strong>"},{"type":"list","items":["<em>Ultimate Sound Design: Mastering Sound Design</em>, Section 6.14 (Sample &amp; Hold Modular FX)"],"ordered":false}]}],"minutes":8},{"id":"sounddesign-l-7-2-8","title":"7.2.8 — Saving All 4 to User Library","blocks":[{"type":"step","title":"7.2.8 — Saving All 4 to User Library","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"Save each created patch to <code>Presets/Instruments/Analog/</code>:"},{"type":"list","items":["<code>SD_Analog_DetroitTechnoStab.adv</code>","<code>SD_Analog_VintageReeseBass.adv</code>","<code>SD_Analog_80sPolyBrass.adv</code>","<code>SD_Analog_SH_ComputerChatterFX.adv</code>"],"ordered":true},{"type":"prose","html":"<strong>Sources:</strong>"},{"type":"list","items":["<em>Sound-Design-Beginner-Knowledgebase.md</em>, §11 &amp; §14"],"ordered":false}]}],"minutes":8}],"exercises":[]},{"id":"sounddesign-m-7-3-3","code":"7.3","title":"Four Design Decisions","blurb":"- Apply the Four Design Decisions Framework to diagnose, structure, and synthesize any target sound from scratch. - Translate abstract acoustic references and mental targets into four deterministic parameter domains:","outcomes":[],"sources":[],"practiceFocus":[],"checkpoints":[],"lessons":[{"id":"sounddesign-l-o-21","title":"Section overview","blocks":[{"type":"step","title":"Learning Objectives","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["Apply the <strong>Four Design Decisions Framework</strong> to diagnose, structure, and synthesize any target sound from scratch.","Translate abstract acoustic references and mental targets into four deterministic parameter domains:","<strong>Thick vs. Thin</strong> (Oscillator count, Unison, Detuning)","<strong>High vs. Low</strong> (Octave transposition, Filter Cutoff floor)","<strong>Short vs. Long</strong> (Amplitude &amp; Filter Envelope Decay / Sustain)","<strong>Motion vs. Still</strong> (LFO rates, Envelopes, Mod Wheel assignments)","Execute the four-step <strong>Preset Reverse-Engineering Protocol</strong>: Hear → Predict → Open → Verify.","Conduct a \"Why Audit\" justifying every active parameter position on a finished patch."],"ordered":false}]}],"minutes":8},{"id":"sounddesign-l-7-3-1","title":"7.3.1 — Decision 1: Thick vs. Thin","blocks":[{"type":"step","title":"7.3.1 — Decision 1: Thick vs. Thin","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"Before touching a single knob, evaluate the required spatial and harmonic density:"},{"type":"prose","html":"<pre style=\"white-space:pre-wrap;font-family:ui-monospace,monospace\">+------------------+----------------------------------+-----------------------------------------+\n| Decision Target  | Synthesis Parameter Decisions    | Sound Design Role                       |\n+------------------+----------------------------------+-----------------------------------------+\n| **THICK**        | - Multiple oscillators ($2 - 3$) | Supersaw chords, wide atmospheric pads, |\n| (Dense / Wide)   | - Micro-detune ($\\pm 8-15\\text{c}$)| heavy Reese basses, ensemble leads.   |\n|                  | - Unison Mode ($4 - 8\\text{ voices}$)|                                       |\n|                  | - Wide stereo pan spread         |                                         |\n+------------------+----------------------------------+-----------------------------------------+\n| **THIN**         | - Single oscillator ($1\\text{ voice}$)| Pure sub-bass fundamentals, tight     |\n| (Focused / Mono) | - Zero detune                    | staccato plucks, clinical acid lines,   |\n|                  | - Unison Off (Mono Voice Mode)   | rhythmic arpeggiator sequences.         |\n|                  | - Centered mono spatial profile  |                                         |\n+------------------+----------------------------------+-----------------------------------------+</pre>"},{"type":"prose","html":"<pre style=\"white-space:pre-wrap;font-family:ui-monospace,monospace\">THIN (Single Focused Voice):          THICK (Multi-Voice Detuned Unison):\n     [ Center Core ]                        \\    \\   |   /    /\n                                           [ Wide Stereo Spread ]</pre>"},{"type":"prose","html":"<strong>Sources:</strong>"},{"type":"list","items":["<em>Ultimate Sound Design: Mastering Sound Design</em>, Section 7.1 (The Density Axis)","<em>Sound-Design-Beginner-Knowledgebase.md</em>, §2 &amp; §5"],"ordered":false}]}],"minutes":8},{"id":"sounddesign-l-7-3-2","title":"7.3.2 — Decision 2: High vs. Low","blocks":[{"type":"step","title":"7.3.2 — Decision 2: High vs. Low","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"Determine the target sound's spectral register across the frequency arrangement:"},{"type":"prose","html":"<pre style=\"white-space:pre-wrap;font-family:ui-monospace,monospace\">+------------------+----------------------------------+-----------------------------------------+\n| Decision Target  | Synthesis Parameter Decisions    | Acoustic Frequency Band                 |\n+------------------+----------------------------------+-----------------------------------------+\n| **LOW**          | - Oscillator Octave: $-1\\text{ or } -2$| $30\\text{ Hz} - 250\\text{ Hz}$          |\n| (Sub / Bass)     | - Filter Cutoff: $150\\text{ Hz} - 500\\text{ Hz}$| Pure low-end foundation;        |\n|                  | - Filter Keytracking: $0\\%$      | preserves headroom for the mix.         |\n+------------------+----------------------------------+-----------------------------------------+\n| **HIGH**         | - Oscillator Octave: $0\\text{ or } +1$| $1.5\\text{ kHz} - 16\\text{ kHz}$        |\n| (Lead / Sizzle)  | - Filter Cutoff: Open ($&gt;4\\text{ kHz}$)| Piercing clarity, air, and bite;  |\n|                  | - High-Pass Filter: Cut below $200\\text{ Hz}$| cuts through dense arrangements.  |\n+------------------+----------------------------------+-----------------------------------------+</pre>"},{"type":"prose","html":"<strong>Sources:</strong>"},{"type":"list","items":["<em>Ultimate Sound Design: Mastering Sound Design</em>, Section 7.2 (The Register Axis)"],"ordered":false}]}],"minutes":8},{"id":"sounddesign-l-7-3-3","title":"7.3.3 — Decision 3: Short vs. Long","blocks":[{"type":"step","title":"7.3.3 — Decision 3: Short vs. Long","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"Establish the temporal role of the sound using ADSR envelope calibration:"},{"type":"prose","html":"<pre style=\"white-space:pre-wrap;font-family:ui-monospace,monospace\">+------------------+----------------------------------+-----------------------------------------+\n| Decision Target  | Envelope Calibration (AEG &amp; FEG) | Musical Behavior                        |\n+------------------+----------------------------------+-----------------------------------------+\n| **SHORT**        | - Attack: $0\\text{ ms} - 2\\text{ ms}$| Staccato plucks, percussion, punchy     |\n| (Percussive)     | - Decay: $80\\text{ ms} - 350\\text{ ms}$| synth keys, 808 transient clicks.     |\n|                  | - Sustain: **$0.0$ ($-\\text{inf}$)**| Note dies out immediately even if key   |\n|                  | - Release: $20\\text{ ms} - 80\\text{ ms}$| is held down.                         |\n+------------------+----------------------------------+-----------------------------------------+\n| **LONG**         | - Attack: $200\\text{ ms} - 2.0\\text{ s}$| Sustained chords, evolving pads,        |\n| (Sustained)      | - Decay: $1.0\\text{ s} - 3.0\\text{ s}$| drone foundations, ambient soundscapes. |\n|                  | - Sustain: **$75\\% - 100\\%$**    | Note sustains continuously as long as   |\n|                  | - Release: $1.0\\text{ s} - 5.0\\text{ s}$| key is held; rings out on release.     |\n+------------------+----------------------------------+-----------------------------------------+</pre>"},{"type":"prose","html":"<strong>Sources:</strong>"},{"type":"list","items":["<em>Ultimate Sound Design: Mastering Sound Design</em>, Section 7.3 (The Temporal Axis)","<em>Sound-Design-Beginner-Knowledgebase.md</em>, §3"],"ordered":false}]}],"minutes":8},{"id":"sounddesign-l-7-3-4","title":"7.3.4 — Decision 4: Motion vs. Still","blocks":[{"type":"step","title":"7.3.4 — Decision 4: Motion vs. Still","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"Dictate the dynamic evolution of the sound over time:"},{"type":"prose","html":"<pre style=\"white-space:pre-wrap;font-family:ui-monospace,monospace\">+------------------+----------------------------------+-----------------------------------------+\n| Decision Target  | Modulation Routing               | Acoustic Experience                     |\n+------------------+----------------------------------+-----------------------------------------+\n| **STILL**        | - LFOs Disabled ($0\\%$ Depth)    | Pure, stable, clinical precision.       |\n| (Static)         | - Static Filter Cutoff           | Anchored sub-basses, fixed organ stabs, |\n|                  | - Unmodulated Pitch              | laser-focused synth punches.            |\n+------------------+----------------------------------+-----------------------------------------+\n| **MOTION**       | - Tempo-Synced LFOs ($1/8, 1/4$) | Pulsing wobbles, rhythmic tremolos,     |\n| (Dynamic)        | - Slow Free LFOs ($0.2\\text{ Hz}$)| sweeping formants, expressive vibrato,  |\n|                  | - Velocity &amp; Mod Wheel Mappings  | live performance evolution.             |\n+------------------+----------------------------------+-----------------------------------------+</pre>"},{"type":"prose","html":"<strong>Sources:</strong>"},{"type":"list","items":["<em>Ultimate Sound Design: Mastering Sound Design</em>, Section 7.4 (The Modulation Axis)","<em>Sound-Design-Beginner-Knowledgebase.md</em>, §4"],"ordered":false}]}],"minutes":8},{"id":"sounddesign-l-7-3-5","title":"7.3.5 — Translating Reference Sounds into the 4 Decisions","blocks":[{"type":"step","title":"7.3.5 — Translating Reference Sounds into the 4 Decisions","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"Whenever you hear a sound in a commercial track or reference recording, translate it into the Four Decisions Matrix:"},{"type":"prose","html":"<pre style=\"white-space:pre-wrap;font-family:ui-monospace,monospace\">+---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------+\n|                               FOUR-DECISION TRANSLATION EXAMPLES                                  |\n+---------------------+-------------------+-------------------+-----------------+-------------------+\n| Reference Sound     | 1. Thick / Thin   | 2. High / Low     | 3. Short / Long | 4. Motion / Still |\n+---------------------+-------------------+-------------------+-----------------+-------------------+\n| **Sub Bass**        | **Thin** (1 Osc)  | **Low** (-1 Oct)  | **Long** (Held) | **Still** (Static)|\n+---------------------+-------------------+-------------------+-----------------+-------------------+\n| **Dubstep Wub**     | **Thick** (2 Osc) | **Low-Mid** (Mid) | **Long** (Held) | **Motion** (LFO)  |\n+---------------------+-------------------+-------------------+-----------------+-------------------+\n| **Trance Pluck**    | **Thick** (Unison)| **Mid-High** (0st)| **Short** (D=200ms)| **Still** (Env) |\n+---------------------+-------------------+-------------------+-----------------+-------------------+\n| **Ambient Pad**     | **Thick** (Detune)| **High** (+1 Oct) | **Long** (Slow A)| **Motion** (Slow LFO)|\n+---------------------+-------------------+-------------------+-----------------+-------------------+</pre>"},{"type":"prose","html":"Once the four matrix boxes are filled, you know the exact initial parameter states to set on any synthesizer before you begin fine-tuning."},{"type":"prose","html":"<strong>Sources:</strong>"},{"type":"list","items":["<em>Ultimate Sound Design: Mastering Sound Design</em>, Section 7.5 (Diagnostic Matrix)"],"ordered":false}]}],"minutes":8},{"id":"sounddesign-l-7-3-6","title":"7.3.6 — Preset Reverse-Engineering Protocol","blocks":[{"type":"step","title":"7.3.6 — Preset Reverse-Engineering Protocol","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"To learn from existing factory presets without becoming dependent on them, follow the <strong>Four-Step Reverse-Engineering Protocol</strong>:"},{"type":"prose","html":"<pre style=\"white-space:pre-wrap;font-family:ui-monospace,monospace\">+-------------------------------------------------------------------------------+\n|                   FOUR-STEP REVERSE-ENGINEERING PROTOCOL                      |\n+-------------------------------------------------------------------------------+\n| 1. HEAR:    Listen to the preset in solo. Analyze it across the 4 Decisions.   |\n| 2. PREDICT: Guess the oscillator waveforms, filter cutoff/slope, and envelope |\n|             decay times before opening the device interface.                  |\n| 3. OPEN:    Open the synth GUI. Inspect the actual parameter settings.        |\n| 4. VERIFY:  Mute sections one-by-one (turn off Filter, turn off Osc 2, turn  |\n|             off FX) to hear the isolated contribution of each component.      |\n+-------------------------------------------------------------------------------+</pre>"},{"type":"prose","html":"<strong>Sources:</strong>"},{"type":"list","items":["<em>Ultimate Sound Design: Mastering Sound Design</em>, Section 7.6 (Reverse-Engineering Methodology)"],"ordered":false}]}],"minutes":8},{"id":"sounddesign-l-7-3-7","title":"7.3.7 — The \"Why Audit\"","blocks":[{"type":"step","title":"7.3.7 — The \"Why Audit\"","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"When authoring or verifying any patch, perform a <strong>Why Audit</strong>: point to every active knob on the interface and state its exact physical justification:"},{"type":"list","items":["<em>\"Why is Osc 2 detuned by +8 cents?\"</em> → <em>\"To create a 16 cent phase beating cycle that thickens the midrange.\"</em>","<em>\"Why is the Filter Cutoff set to 350 Hz?\"</em> → <em>\"To eliminate harsh high-frequency harmonics and leave room for the lead vocal.\"</em>","<em>\"Why is Amp Sustain set to 0.0?\"</em> → <em>\"To enforce a percussive pluck contour regardless of how long the key is held.\"</em>"],"ordered":false},{"type":"prose","html":"If you cannot explain <em>why</em> a parameter is set to its current position, initialize that control to zero."},{"type":"prose","html":"<strong>Sources:</strong>"},{"type":"list","items":["<em>Ultimate Sound Design: Mastering Sound Design</em>, Section 7.7 (The Intentionality Audit)"],"ordered":false}]}],"minutes":8},{"id":"sounddesign-l-7-3-8","title":"7.3.8 — Reverse-Engineering Lab Drill","blocks":[{"type":"step","title":"7.3.8 — Reverse-Engineering Lab Drill","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"<strong>Hands-On Lab:</strong>"},{"type":"list","items":["Open Ableton Live. Load <code>Analog</code>.","Open the factory browser and load any complex factory lead or bass preset.","Apply the 4-step protocol:","Identify the 4 Decisions by ear.","Deconstruct the signal chain: Mute Filter 2 → Mute Osc 2 → Disable LFO 1.","Rebuild the core patch from an initialized <code>Analog</code> template without looking back at the factory preset."],"ordered":true},{"type":"prose","html":"<strong>Sources:</strong>"},{"type":"list","items":["<em>Sound-Design-Beginner-Knowledgebase.md</em>, §11 &amp; §15"],"ordered":false}]}],"minutes":8}],"exercises":[]},{"id":"sounddesign-m-7-4-4","code":"7.4","title":"Engine Chooser","blurb":"- Select the optimal synthesis engine for any sound design task using the Engine Chooser Decision Matrix. - Identify the signature structural strengths of Ableton Operator, Vital, and Surge XT.","outcomes":[],"sources":[],"practiceFocus":[],"checkpoints":[],"lessons":[{"id":"sounddesign-l-o-22","title":"Section overview","blocks":[{"type":"step","title":"Learning Objectives","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["Select the optimal synthesis engine for any sound design task using the <strong>Engine Chooser Decision Matrix</strong>.","Identify the signature structural strengths of <strong>Ableton Operator</strong>, <strong>Vital</strong>, and <strong>Surge XT</strong>.","Recognize when simple subtractive trainers (<code>Drift</code>, <code>Analog</code>) are more efficient than multi-engine workstations.","Recreate a standardized bass motif across Operator, Vital, and Surge XT to evaluate comparative workflow and timbral character.","Complete the <strong>Gate E Self-Assessment Checkpoint</strong>."],"ordered":false}]}],"minutes":8},{"id":"sounddesign-l-7-4-1","title":"7.4.1 — The Core Three: Operator vs. Vital vs. Surge XT","blocks":[{"type":"step","title":"7.4.1 — The Core Three: Operator vs. Vital vs. Surge XT","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"This studio is anchored by three world-class, production-standard synthesis engines. Each engine excels in a distinct acoustic domain:"},{"type":"prose","html":"<pre style=\"white-space:pre-wrap;font-family:ui-monospace,monospace\">+---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------+\n|                                  THE THREE CORE ENGINES MAP                                       |\n+-------------------+-----------------------------+-------------------------------------------------+\n| SYNTHESIS ENGINE  | PRIMARY ARCHITECTURE        | SIGNATURE SOUND DESIGN STRENGTHS                |\n+-------------------+-----------------------------+-------------------------------------------------+\n| **Operator**      | 4-Operator FM / PM,         | Razor-sharp FM house plucks, metallic FM basses,|\n| (Ableton Add-on)  | 64-Partial Additive Editor, | additive organ drawbars, fixed-frequency        |\n|                   | Fixed-Frequency Mode        | synthetic kicks, snares, and acoustic bells.    |\n+-------------------+-----------------------------+-------------------------------------------------+\n| **Vital**         | 3D Wavetable Scanning,      | Heavy modern dubstep growls, vocoded formant    |\n| (VST3 Workstation)| Spectral Warping Domain,    | warps, 16-voice detuned supersaws, instantaneous|\n|                   | Drag-and-Drop Matrix        | visual drag-and-drop modulation workflow.       |\n+-------------------+-----------------------------+-------------------------------------------------+\n| **Surge XT**      | Dual-Scene Multi-Engine,    | Hybrid analog modeling, audio-rate modulation,  |\n| (Open-Source VST3)| Dual Filter Matrix,         | multi-stage waveshaping, massive Reese basses,  |\n|                   | Deep Routing Bar Modulators | complex multi-timbral split/layer soundscapes.  |\n+-------------------+-----------------------------+-------------------------------------------------+</pre>"},{"type":"prose","html":"<strong>Sources:</strong>"},{"type":"list","items":["<em>Sound Design Foundations Syllabus</em>, §0.2 &amp; §11 (Engine Chooser Card)","<em>Sound-Design-Beginner-Knowledgebase.md</em>, §6 &amp; §7"],"ordered":false}]}],"minutes":8},{"id":"sounddesign-l-7-4-2","title":"7.4.2 — When to Choose Operator","blocks":[{"type":"step","title":"7.4.2 — When to Choose Operator","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"Choose <strong>Ableton Operator</strong> when your target sound demands:"},{"type":"list","items":["<strong>Mathematical FM Purity:</strong> Clean integer frequency ratios (1:1, 2:1, 3:1) that generate crisp, bright, metallic sidebands without wavetable artifacts.","<strong>Fixed-Frequency Transients:</strong> Decoupling an oscillator from keyboard pitch tracking (Fixed Mode in Hz) to synthesize consistent synthetic drum transient clicks (1 kHz) across all octaves.","<strong>Additive Partial Editing:</strong> Manually drawing the exact amplitude of up to 64 individual harmonic partials for pure organ drawbars, bells, and custom spectra.","<strong>Native Live Integration:</strong> Ultra-low CPU overhead, instantaneous loading, and perfect Push/Launchkey parameter banking."],"ordered":true},{"type":"prose","html":"<strong>Sources:</strong>"},{"type":"list","items":["<em>Ableton Live 12 Reference Manual</em>, Chapter 23 (Operator Reference)","<em>Sound-Design-Beginner-Knowledgebase.md</em>, §7.2"],"ordered":false}]}],"minutes":8},{"id":"sounddesign-l-7-4-3","title":"7.4.3 — When to Choose Vital","blocks":[{"type":"step","title":"7.4.3 — When to Choose Vital","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"Choose <strong>Vital</strong> when your target sound demands:"},{"type":"list","items":["<strong>Complex Wavetable Morphing:</strong> Smooth 3D interpolation across evolving waveform frames over time.","<strong>Frequency-Domain Spectral Warping:</strong> Altering harmonic bins using <em>Harmonic Stretch</em>, <em>Formant Scale</em>, <em>Vocode</em>, or <em>Inharmonic Smear</em> before filtering.","<strong>Massive Stereo Supersaws:</strong> Stacking up to 16 detuned unison voices per oscillator with dedicated stereo spread and unison phase randomization.","<strong>Fast Visual Modulation:</strong> Dragging an LFO directly onto a knob and visually watching the modulation arc in real time."],"ordered":true},{"type":"prose","html":"<strong>Sources:</strong>"},{"type":"list","items":["<em>Vital User Manual</em>, Section 1 &amp; 2","<em>Sound-Design-Beginner-Knowledgebase.md</em>, §7"],"ordered":false}]}],"minutes":8},{"id":"sounddesign-l-7-4-4","title":"7.4.4 — When to Choose Surge XT","blocks":[{"type":"step","title":"7.4.4 — When to Choose Surge XT","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"Choose <strong>Surge XT</strong> when your target sound demands:"},{"type":"list","items":["<strong>Dual-Scene Multi-Timbral Layering:</strong> Running Scene A and Scene B independently (layering a complex digital FM pluck on top of a warm virtual analog sub).","<strong>Exotic Oscillator Algorithms:</strong> Accessing dedicated <em>String</em> (Karplus-Strong physical modeling), <em>Twist</em> (Mutable Instruments Plaits macro-oscillator), <em>FM2/FM3</em>, and <em>Audio Input</em> processors.","<strong>Complex Dual-Filter Configurations:</strong> Routing two independent multi-mode filters in <em>Serial</em>, <em>Parallel</em>, <em>Stereo Wide</em>, or <em>Ring Modulation</em> with integrated multi-curve waveshaping.","<strong>Deep Modulation Bar:</strong> Assigning multiple Voice LFOs and Scene LFOs with custom mathematical curves."],"ordered":true},{"type":"prose","html":"<strong>Sources:</strong>"},{"type":"list","items":["<em>Surge XT User Manual</em>, Section 1–4","<em>Sound-Design-Beginner-Knowledgebase.md</em>, §7"],"ordered":false}]}],"minutes":8},{"id":"sounddesign-l-7-4-5","title":"7.4.5 — When to Stay in Drift or Analog","blocks":[{"type":"step","title":"7.4.5 — When to Stay in Drift or Analog","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"Do not automatically open a complex multi-engine workstation when a basic tool achieves the goal faster:"},{"type":"list","items":["Choose <strong><code>Drift</code></strong> for rapid single/dual-oscillator subtractive sketches, quick 808 subs, instant laser sweeps, and CPU-efficient tracking.","Choose <strong><code>Analog</code></strong> when you specifically require classic virtual-analog dual-filter serial/parallel routing and dedicated per-oscillator sub-oscillators."],"ordered":false},{"type":"prose","html":"<strong>The Speed Rule:</strong> If a sound requires only two saw waves, a 24 dB low-pass filter, and an ADSR envelope, building it in <code>Drift</code> takes 10 seconds and consumes 0.2% CPU."},{"type":"prose","html":"<strong>Sources:</strong>"},{"type":"list","items":["<em>Sound-Design-Beginner-Knowledgebase.md</em>, §6.2"],"ordered":false}]}],"minutes":8},{"id":"sounddesign-l-7-4-6","title":"7.4.6 — The Engine Chooser Decision Matrix","blocks":[{"type":"step","title":"7.4.6 — The Engine Chooser Decision Matrix","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"Use this deterministic flowchart to select your engine:"},{"type":"prose","html":"<pre style=\"white-space:pre-wrap;font-family:ui-monospace,monospace\">                                  [ TARGET SOUND REQUIREMENT ]\n                                               |\n         +--------------------+----------------+--------------------+--------------------+\n         |                    |                                     |                    |\n         v                    v                                     v                    v\n  [ NEED FM RATIOS /   [ NEED WAVETABLE SCAN /              [ NEED DUAL SCENE /   [ NEED IMMEDIATE   ]\n    ADDITIVE PARTIALS /  SPECTRAL WARPING /                   DUAL FILTERS /       SUBTRACTIVE SKETCH]\n    FIXED DRUM HZ ]      VISUAL MATRIX ]                      HYBRID MODELS ]            |\n         |                    |                                     |                    v\n         v                    v                                     v             [ DRIFT / ANALOG ]\n   [ OPERATOR ]            [ VITAL ]                           [ SURGE XT ]</pre>"},{"type":"prose","html":"<pre style=\"white-space:pre-wrap;font-family:ui-monospace,monospace\">+------------------------------------+------------------------------------------+\n| Sonic Requirement                  | Selected Engine                          |\n+------------------------------------+------------------------------------------+\n| Metallic House Pluck / Donk        | **Operator** (Algorithm 4-op FM)         |\n| Aggressive Bass Growl / Wobble     | **Vital** (Wavetable Morph + Formant)    |\n| Deep Detuned Jungle Reese Bass     | **Surge XT** (Classic Saws + Waveshaper) |\n| Synthetic 808 Sub / Sine Kick      | **Drift** (Fast Pitch Env + Sine)        |\n| 16-Voice Euphoric Supersaw Chord   | **Vital** (16-Voice Unison + Spread)     |\n| Karplus-Strong Plucked Acoustic Str| **Surge XT** (String Oscillator Model)   |\n| Pure 60s Tonewheel Organ Drawbars  | **Operator** (Additive Partial Editor)   |\n+------------------------------------+------------------------------------------+</pre>"},{"type":"prose","html":"<strong>Sources:</strong>"},{"type":"list","items":["<em>Sound Design Foundations Syllabus</em>, §11 (Engine Chooser Card)"],"ordered":false}]}],"minutes":8},{"id":"sounddesign-l-7-4-7","title":"7.4.7 — Cross-Engine Comparison Lab: Same Bass Motif","blocks":[{"type":"step","title":"7.4.7 — Cross-Engine Comparison Lab: Same Bass Motif","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"To understand how each engine imparts its own distinct acoustic character, build the exact same bass patch across all three core engines:"}]},{"type":"step","title":"The Benchmark Patch Spec:","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["<strong>Oscillators:</strong> 2 Sawtooth Waves, Detuned by ± 6 cents, Octave -1.","<strong>Filter:</strong> Low-Pass 24 dB, Cutoff 350 Hz, Resonance 20%.","<strong>Amp Envelope:</strong> Attack 0 ms, Decay 350 ms, Sustain 0.40, Release 30 ms.","<strong>Filter Envelope:</strong> Attack 0 ms, Decay 180 ms, Sustain 0.0, Amount +60%."],"ordered":false}]},{"type":"step","title":"Engine Observations:","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["<strong><code>Operator</code> (Built via Alg 11 / Parallel Saws):</strong> Ultra-punchy, surgical, clinical digital transients.","<strong><code>Vital</code> (Built via Osc 1 + Osc 2 Init Saws):</strong> Wide, modern, crisp top-end shimmer with smooth visual feedback.","<strong><code>Surge XT</code> (Built via Scene A Classic Saws):</strong> Warm, thick, organic virtual analog weight with aggressive filter resonance bite."],"ordered":true},{"type":"prose","html":"<strong>Sources:</strong>"},{"type":"list","items":["<em>Sound-Design-Beginner-Knowledgebase.md</em>, §11 &amp; §15"],"ordered":false}]}],"minutes":8},{"id":"sounddesign-l-7-4-8","title":"7.4.8 — Gate E: Self-Assessment Checkpoint","blocks":[{"type":"step","title":"7.4.8 — Gate E: Self-Assessment Checkpoint","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"Before advancing to <strong>Module 8 (Operator in Full)</strong>, verify your subtractive mastery and engine selection competencies:"},{"type":"prose","html":"<pre style=\"white-space:pre-wrap;font-family:ui-monospace,monospace\">+-------------------------------------------------------------------------------+\n|                             GATE E CHECKLIST                                  |\n+-------------------------------------------------------------------------------+\n| [ ] 1. Subtractive Patch Construction:                                        |\n|     Can you build a Bass, Lead, Pluck, and Pad completely from scratch on an |\n|     initialized Drift or Analog without using presets or tutorial videos?     |\n|                                                                               |\n| [ ] 2. The Four Decisions Translation:                                        |\n|     Can you listen to an unfamiliar reference sound and immediately identify  |\n|     its state across Thick/Thin, High/Low, Short/Long, and Motion/Still?      |\n|                                                                               |\n| [ ] 3. Engine Chooser Justification:                                          |\n|     Can you explain in technical terms why you would select Operator for an   |\n|     FM house pluck, Vital for a wavetable growl, and Surge XT for a dual-scene|\n|     hybrid reese?                                                             |\n|                                                                               |\n| [ ] 4. Reverse-Engineering Verification:                                      |\n|     Can you execute the 4-step protocol on a complex factory preset, identify |\n|     its signal chain, and explain why every active parameter is set where it is?|\n|                                                                               |\n| [ ] 5. Library Archiving:                                                     |\n|     Are your custom user patches saved and organized with standard taxonomy   |\n|     inside your Ableton User Library?                                         |\n+-------------------------------------------------------------------------------+</pre>"},{"type":"prose","html":"If all 5 criteria are verified by ear and manual execution, <strong>Gate E is passed</strong>. You have achieved full subtractive synthesis literacy. Proceed to <strong>Module 8: Operator in Full</strong>."},{"type":"prose","html":"<strong>Sources:</strong>"},{"type":"list","items":["<em>Sound Design Foundations Syllabus</em>, §3 (Gate E Definition)"],"ordered":false}]}],"minutes":8}],"exercises":[]}]};
